From 82e2623d98e764f7951671734b6759ff41c023d2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ming Lei <ming.lei@clyso.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 00:52:50 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] osd, mon: pool flag read_fadvise_dontneed

Object data a client reads once still goes into the OSD's buffer cache
(bluestore_default_buffered_read), and the memory tuner then gives that
cache whatever has been touched recently, up to the memory target.  On
a pool of large objects that are read in whole and rarely again, such as
an RGW data pool of a file-sharing or backup service, that is memory
spent on data with no second use.

A pool flag already exists for the write side: write_fadvise_dontneed
adds FADVISE_DONTNEED to every write of the pool.  Add the read side,
read_fadvise_dontneed: the primary adds FADVISE_DONTNEED to the client's
read, sync read, sparse read, checksum, cmpext and copy-get ops of the
pool (and so to the reads a class method makes, which go through the
same code), and the object store serves them without keeping the data.
The flag reaches BlueStore on every read path: ReplicatedBackend passes
the op flags to the store, and both EC backends carry them in the shard
reads.  A client that hints FADVISE_WILLNEED still gets its data cached,
as BlueStore checks that first.  Buffers already in the cache are still
used.

The flag is off by default and set per pool by the operator.

Measured with warp against RGW on six NVMe OSDs (osd_memory_target
4 GiB), 180 s phases of PUT 4 MiB, PUT 4 KiB, GET 4 MiB and GET 4 KiB
on a replicated size 3 and on an EC 4+2 data pool, the same binary with
the flag off and on the data pool.  The highest OSD peak RSS of the GET
phases falls from 2.8-3.2 GiB to 1.3-1.7 GiB, the buffer cache from
1.5-1.9 GiB to nothing, and GET throughput and latency stay within the
run-to-run spread (GET 4 MiB: 2622 -> 3066 MiB/s replicated, 2197 ->
2246 MiB/s EC; GET 4 KiB and both PUT phases within 8%).  The benchmark
re-reads a set of 2,500 objects, so the cache the flag gives up was
serving 63% (replicated) and 86% (EC) of the read bytes, and the mean
BlueStore read latency rose from 0.3 to 0.8 ms (replicated) and from
0.1 to 0.4 ms (EC); an RGW GET of 84-116 ms end to end does not show
that.  Setting bluestore_default_buffered_read = false instead gives the
same peak, and in one pair of runs per pool 4-5% less GET 4 MiB
throughput, within the spread of the setup; the flag's advantage is that
it changes one pool and leaves the rest as they are.

On an RBD pool of four 32 GiB images (fio on librbd, same six OSDs) the
buffer cache served under 1% of the read bytes, as 1.1 GiB of cache per
OSD cannot hold 64 GiB of replicas; with the flag on the pool the read
phases' highest peak RSS falls from 2.5-3.1 GiB to 1.4-1.6 GiB, 4 KiB
random reads go from 166-171k to 190-195k IOPS (two runs each, the
OSDs' mean read latency 0.19 -> 0.16 ms, consistent with less cache churn
for no hits)
and 4 MiB reads and the writes stay within the spread.

The flag is added to the OSDOp's flags in place, as write_fadvise_dontneed
does, so it is also visible in the ops of the reply.

Signed-off-by: Ming Lei <ming.lei@clyso.com>
